{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "weno5_eno1_blend_D1y_interior",
    "description": "Interior-only hybrid WENO5/ENO1 upwind first derivative d u/dy of NCAR fire_behavior's fire_upwinding = 10 -- the same scheme as fire_upwinding = 9 but with a BLENDING ZONE instead of a hard band edge -- on cartesian_uniform_2d over the block [3, NX-2] x [4, NY-3]. It is stencils/weno5_eno1_D1y_interior with the band switch replaced by a smooth partition of unity and nothing else. The reference writes it as three branches on a = |u[i,j]| with band_width = 2 B and transition_width = B, B = weno_band_cells dx: mask = 1 for a <= B, mask = 0.5 (1 + cos(pi (a - B)/B)) for B < a <= 2B, and mask = 0 for a > 2B; then diff2 = mask diff2_weno + (1 - mask) diff2_eno (stencils/weno5_eno1_cosine_blend). Here that is one closed expression, mask = 0.5 (1 + cos(pi min(max((a - B)/B, 0), 1))), which is EXACTLY equivalent because the clamp saturates the cosine's argument at 0 and pi at the two ends -- no branching, per AGENTS.md 1. The wall test is unchanged and still hard: a cell within bdy_eno1_cells of any wall takes ENO1 outright, because case 10 sits inside the same wall `else` as cases 8 and 9. Unlike the reference, which skips the WENO5 evaluation where mask = 0, this evaluates it and multiplies by zero; that is safe because the Jiang-Shu weights are eps-regularised and finite everywhere. Upwind side and free names are as in the fire_upwinding = 9 sibling: the wind-relative propagation sign from wind_u, wind_v. Free names: wind_u, wind_v, bdy_eno1_cells, weno_band_cells, pi_blend, dx, dy. pi_blend is pi; it is a free name rather than a literal so the consumer supplies it from its own constants library.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:stencil",
      "family:finite_difference",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d",
      "op:D",
      "axes:x,y",
      "order:1",
      "stencil_width:7"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Munoz-Esparza, D., Kosovic, B., Jimenez, P. A., & Coen, J. L. (2018). An accurate fire-spread algorithm in the Weather Research and Forecasting model using the level-set method. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(4), 908-926. The hybrid WENO5/ENO1 level-set scheme (fire_upwinding = 9) of WRF-Fire / NCAR fire_behavior.",
        "doi": "10.1002/2017MS001108"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jiang, G.-S., & Shu, C.-W. (1996). Efficient implementation of weighted ENO schemes. Journal of Computational Physics, 126(1), 202-228. The classical WENO5 smoothness indicators beta_k and nonlinear weights w_k = gamma_k/(beta_k + eps)^2.",
        "doi": "10.1006/jcph.1996.0130"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Harten, A., Engquist, B., Osher, S., & Chakravarthy, S. R. (1987). Uniformly high order accurate essentially non-oscillatory schemes, III. Journal of Computational Physics, 71(2), 231-303. ENO stencil selection by smallest divided difference (first-order form: the minmod limiter).",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(87)90031-3"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR Community Fire Behavior Model (fire_behavior), develop @ eb77580, physics/level_set_mod.F90: Calc_tend_ls, Select_eno, Select_weno5, Select_4th, Select_godunov (case 4), Extrapol_var_at_bdys. The transcribed reference implementation."
      }
    ]
  },
